# Market-Implied Equilibrium Returns

This notebook computes equilibrium expected returns implied by
market portfolio weights and the covariance matrix.

These returns represent the market consensus before any investor views
are introduced.

import pandas as pd

from src.data_loader import load_price_data, compute_returns
from src.covariance import shrinkage_covariance
from src.equilibrium import implied_equilibrium_returns

tickers = ["RELIANCE.NS", "TCS.NS", "INFY.NS", "HDFCBANK.NS", "ICICIBANK.NS"]
start_date = "2019-01-01"
end_date = "2024-12-31"

prices = load_price_data(tickers, start_date, end_date)
returns = compute_returns(prices)

cov_matrix = shrinkage_covariance(returns)

n = len(tickers)
market_weights = pd.Series([1/n] * n, index=tickers)
market_weights

pi = implied_equilibrium_returns(cov_matrix, market_weights)
pi

historical_returns = returns.mean() * 252

pd.DataFrame({
    "Historical Mean Returns": historical_returns,
    "Equilibrium Returns": pi
})


## Key Insight

Historical mean returns are noisy and unstable.
Market-implied equilibrium returns are smoother because they are derived
from the risk the market is already holding.

Black–Litterman uses these equilibrium returns as a neutral starting point
before incorporating subjective investor views.
